<%# NO .page wrapper of its own, deliberately -- same reason as
    pass_revoked.html.erb, which this view is modelled on: render_finished_passing
    renders this under the APPLICATION layout, which has already opened
    <div class="page">, and .page carries min-height: 100svh, so a second one
    nested inside it stacks a full viewport below the sticky topbar. See the
    pass_revoked.html.erb comment for the measured numbers; this view was
    checked the same way before it shipped. %>
<h2><%= t("game_passings.gated_finish.title") %></h2>

<%# No "unplaced" fallback: render_finished_passing serves this screen only
    for a COMPLETED attempt, and a completed attempt is in pass_standings by
    construction, so @place is always set. The label that stood here was
    reachable only through the quit path this branch used to render -- a
    string nothing legitimate produced. %>
<p><%= t("game_passings.gated_finish.your_place") %>: <%= @place %></p>
<p><%= t("game_passings.gated_finish.your_time") %>:
   <%= @game_passing.seconds_to_hms(@game_passing.duration) %></p>

<% if @ledger.any? %>
  <h3><%= t("game_passings.gated_finish.your_points") %></h3>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="table--cards">
      <tbody>
        <% @ledger.each do |row| %>
          <tr>
            <%# The reason, then whatever identifies THIS row among rows with
                the same reason -- the level for an award or a fine, the
                operator's note for an adjustment. The two are mutually
                exclusive: PointTransaction.award! never writes a note and
                .adjust! always leaves level_id nil.
                Without them, two "Очко за уровень | 10" rows for different
                levels read identically (although @ledger already eager-loads
                includes(:level)), and an adjustment reads "Корректировка |
                33" -- an unexplained plus-or-minus N on the one screen a
                customer reads for their points, which is where they stop
                reading and contact support instead. The note is REQUIRED by
                PointTransaction's own validation, so it is always there to
                show.
                One cell rather than a column of its own: a note column would
                stand empty on every level row, and an empty cell in this
                table is not blank below 48rem -- .table--cards td::before
                prints the column name over it.
                The reason label is translated from a fixed set (an
                unrecognised value must not print "translation missing" to a
                customer); the level name and the note are author- and
                operator-written content, rendered verbatim, never through
                t() and escaped by ERB's default. %>
            <td data-label="<%= t("teams.show.reason") %>">
              <%= PointTransaction::REASONS.include?(row.reason) ? t("teams.show.reasons.#{row.reason}") : row.reason %>
              <% if row.level %>— <%= row.level.name %><% end %>
              <% if row.note.present? %>— <%= row.note %><% end %>
            </td>
            <td data-label="<%= t("teams.show.amount") %>"><%= row.amount %></td>
          </tr>
        <% end %>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p><%= t("game_passings.gated_finish.balance") %>: <%= @game_passing.team.balance %></p>
<% end %>

<%# standings passed in: render_finished_passing already loaded and sorted
    them to work out @place, and the partial would otherwise do both again. %>
<%= render "games/pass_standings", game: @game, standings: @standings, highlight: @game_passing %>

<%# The link a finishing team used to land next to, before this screen
    replaced the redirect to the game page. Same guard and same key as
    show_results.html.erb, so it appears if and only if following it would
    succeed (LogsController#ensure_full_log_access). %>
<% if full_log_visible?(@game) %>
  <p><%= link_to t("game_passings.show_results.full_log"), show_full_log_path(@game) %></p>
<% end %>

<p><%= link_to @game.name, game_path(@game) %></p>
